<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leuven</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Louvain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leuven (French: Louvain) is a city in the Belgian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flemish-brabant">Flemish Brabant</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, on the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dyle">Dijle</a>. Its counts became dukes of Brabant in the 12th century, and the city was a centre of the medieval cloth trade. The university founded there in 1425 is the oldest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>; its library was burnt by German troops in 1914 and again in 1940.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leuven.jpg" alt="Leuven" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vandevorst, Kris · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tafelrond_Leuven_-_385956_-_onroerenderfgoed.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brabant grew out of the County of <b>Louvain</b>, a lordship on the Dyle river held from the eleventh century by a line of counts who also controlled Brussels.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The forts of Liège delayed the Germans for some days before falling to heavy siege artillery, and the German advance was marked by massacres of civilians at Dinant and <b>Leuven</b>, where the university library was burned.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…at Basel in 1516; a College of the Three Languages for Latin, Greek and Hebrew was founded at <b>Leuven</b> in 1517; and in 1530 Francis I of France, urged by Guillaume Budé, appointed royal lecturers in Greek and Hebrew in Paris, the beginning of the Collège de France.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The poem was long attributed to Bernard of Clairvaux (1091–1153) and is now attributed to the medieval poet Arnulf of <b>Leuven</b> (died 1250).</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-sacred-head-now-wounded">O Sacred Head, Now Wounded</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1003 Lambert I the Bearded, a member of the Reginarid family, took the title of count of <b>Louvain</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-louvain">County of Louvain</a></li><li><blockquote>Ducal power was always limited, since counts such as those of <b>Louvain</b>, Hainaut, Namur, Holland and Limburg and the prince-bishops of Liège and Utrecht built their own principalities.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-lower-lorraine">Duchy of Lower Lorraine</a></li></ul></section>
